From 613d227da18d1dab7ce7c56c4e2525490e74c3e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 13:52:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09831/11362] [outer-loop] Retire the one-family flagship
 source-shard scope (exp6581, exp6582)

Operator directive: retire both flagship shard tasks to the exclusion
manifest.

Exp6581 (Qwen3.6) and Exp6582 (Gemma-4-31B) reach the identical verdict,
blocked_precondition_failed_without_quality_claim, on two different model
families. Neither fails fast at an admission gate. Both load a model and do
real work first: Exp6581 ran 927s, Exp6582 ran 1109s and then 2411s on its
re-run. Two families failing at the same point implicates the quality
precondition, not either model.

The conductor logged 10 poison-and-rerun events across these two tasks in
four hours. Every part of that is individually correct. The artifact
honestly declares verdict_class blocked, the poison guard correctly refuses
to log a blocked artifact as OK, and it schedules a re-run. The re-run
reproduces the same block. Together they form a loop with no exit, costing
up to 40 minutes of live GPU per iteration. Retirement is the mechanism the
Failed-Experiment Rerun Discipline provides for exactly this shape.

TWO FILES, because there are two manifests and they are not synced. This
matters more than the retirement itself:

  ops/exclusion_manifest.yaml            read by exclusion_manifest_lint.py
                                         at ACTIVATION -- blocks a future
                                         roadmap, does nothing to a running
                                         one.
  scripts/conductor_exclusion_manifest.json
                                         read by
                                         _ensure_exclusion_manifest_loaded
                                         at RUNTIME, per task launch.

The JSON had not been touched since May 14. Writing only the YAML would have
left the in-flight loop running while looking like the task was retired.
Nothing generates one from the other, and no check compares them.

Worth knowing about the runtime half: _task_is_excluded's own docstring says
"Exclusion is a performance optimisation, not a safety gate", and it returns
(False, "no id") when it cannot extract an integer id or the manifest fails
to load. It fails open. So this retirement stops the loop only while the
JSON parses and the task id keeps its exp<N>- shape.

Verified through the real code path rather than by reading the file:
ExclusionManifest.is_excluded() returns True for 6581 and 6582 and False for
a control id. The activation lint stays clean on the current roadmap.

Scope: this retires the one-family flagship source-shard runs at the current
precondition. It does not retire flagship model use. Reopen once someone
diagnoses why the quality precondition fails after a successful load, and
says what changed. operator_reopen_required is set.

